#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161910 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a3741e-a87c-5b57-95b3-5ef47f7ad599" :question/math-academy-id "e-9907" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\}  = \\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161911 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "06ca6e76-6354-5450-882e-b0c063acd303" :question/math-academy-id "e-9908" :question/problem "![](images/09/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA,$ find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_1|| = 8 = \\sigma_1,$ which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64,$ and\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_2|| = 6 = \\sigma_2,$ which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = 36$ of $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/id #uuid "23c1671b-da2c-570b-af4f-c6aad258155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9909" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4,$ $\\sigma_2=2,$ and $\\sigma_3 = 0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4,$ the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5884f354-e5d3-5445-b252-6177d7fb10d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266935} {:answer/id #uuid "59a2b792-bce2-5ab3-8129-3ed501c97fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266941} {:answer/id #uuid "66a53c17-6cca-59e9-b3a7-10509d1e6f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267055} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed785dc-de4a-56df-9005-91dc3382836b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267261} {:answer/id #uuid "c268b638-dc68-5720-86dc-4bdf86ac9c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59a2b792-bce2-5ab3-8129-3ed501c97fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266941} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f2e673-2942-5733-b153-c9e00dd002b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2e59ffd-322c-5739-bff6-4abbfbcffef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139415" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=5\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef552c8c-90fc-5363-9719-475ae8bd5508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159393} {:answer/id #uuid "3bacc6f5-df88-5c51-93db-5398feefbd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159394} {:answer/id #uuid "4ded2f4b-56dd-5eb4-8982-e9da3ccd4240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159395} {:answer/id #uuid "57e7e213-09d5-55d8-8ba0-5a4bdeeba3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159396} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4f7ae9-1d7b-5020-9811-6832f03131db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159397}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b2fe1e82-1f08-5ac7-9601-b9398a2279f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "843730e5-1d6c-590a-be6f-f5bc76d354a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369715} {:answer/id #uuid "7d127bbd-7566-53f6-8abe-dfa396d7a93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369716} {:answer/id #uuid "9b31420c-6449-52e6-a9be-475a6ec94b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369717} {:answer/id #uuid "b308a8e3-0047-576e-8cfc-a0c1e309ab3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369718} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcdf069-d67b-5c89-b3b7-b34c02297e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d127bbd-7566-53f6-8abe-dfa396d7a93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369716} :answer-field/id #uuid "12561a6a-dd60-5043-b724-be8966244b1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55a74907-e699-576b-a088-5a1093017cc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-139507" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=3,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{2},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=3,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={3}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1121018b-9990-5e8d-875a-4699d6eff1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369736} {:answer/id #uuid "11ca46e8-c877-512b-b761-4afdcf87aea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369737} {:answer/id #uuid "24c9aa0d-688d-5de3-bf46-b7faa7b10dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369738} {:answer/id #uuid "779e6a3e-9b87-50db-92fd-b72e345a265b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369739} {:answer/id #uuid "15aacf44-9909-5b15-91a3-740aeedf74a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24c9aa0d-688d-5de3-bf46-b7faa7b10dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369738} :answer-field/id #uuid "90c9b181-3d57-5309-98db-647cea5eb04e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26241502-86a3-53fb-a4ec-a6023ba45794" :question/math-academy-id "q-139536" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=11$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=11,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={11}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={11}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=11.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2380798b-79b8-5bc8-8153-92b706b0d629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266470} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c7d7d-7d57-554b-8590-31cd03b7bf50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267684} {:answer/id #uuid "bd57370c-6b04-52dc-acbe-5e2a687609ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267839} {:answer/id #uuid "cca90f51-cb57-5f81-bcb5-412e125542b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267976} {:answer/id #uuid "f862619f-07c7-5287-9b92-80801fdc6ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c7d7d-7d57-554b-8590-31cd03b7bf50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267684} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9e107d-8a8c-55a2-9d3c-26e1ff04f4aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f71d275b-8f83-5bf3-90f9-5e8ed34d76ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-139547" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4947e37239d2ac2950b536d7afc2d0810ea713c437b7095a43e123d50ff2b34.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252f6f3b-5a10-5189-8e0c-406c29122f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{A}^{T}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266477} {:answer/id #uuid "77ec066c-ebeb-56f0-86e7-24ebc13114e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267194} {:answer/id #uuid "afcf1cd5-5c2b-558f-b5b8-acc6c19c0e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267727} {:answer/id #uuid "c409ba11-4a9d-56ac-922a-569a66b0dde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267894} {:answer/id #uuid "cf182881-986b-5994-95c4-d3b02929dcab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c409ba11-4a9d-56ac-922a-569a66b0dde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267894} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cec0f57-03b7-5e8d-a4e5-e4bf91fb6e3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f621452-f60c-5e1d-b4df-620aa05fd780" :question/math-academy-id "q-139555" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d1cefc0c789518d21279c5c49a18f80220a2aad80973f0e84fda0ddaf25493b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $100$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=10={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={10}^{2}=100,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=8={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={8}^{2}=64.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=100$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$"}]}
